  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nClouds are clearing this afternoon under west to northwesterly flow, \nwith a hot and humid day developing. Temperatures should jump up \nquickly with sunnier skies, pushing high temperatures into the 80s \nto near 90F with moist dewpoints. A Heat Advisory remains in effect \nfor portions of the area through this evening. \n\nAs an upper level high pushes east overnight, a weak upper level \ndisturbance along its periphery along with a marginal LLJ could \nforce a couple storms out of a well-capped and somewhat dry air mass \nalong the International Border and into the MN Arrowhead. The \nprimary issue here is the actual development of storms, if that can \noccur, decent MUCAPE and ample bulk shear should allow for storms to \ngrow into a respectable elevated storm severe threat, with damaging \nwinds and large hail as the primary hazards. As the LLJ weans into \nSunday morning, any areas of precipitation that do develop should \nweaken or diminish.  \n\nSunday, a hot and moist airmass should push from the Dakotas into \nthe Northland. Widespread high temperatures in the 90s (near 100F in \nthe Brainerd Lakes region) combined with dew points in the upper 60s \nto mid 70s will make for an uncomfortable, and at times somewhat \noppressive, heat. Heat Advisories and Extreme Heat Warnings are in \neffect. With that heat and humidity, an impressive reservoir of \ninstability is expected to develop, with models suggesting 3000-\n4000+ J/kg of MUCAPE across Minnesota, Wisconsin, and the UP. Along \nwith that, around 50 knots of bulk shear could combine with steep \nmid level lapse rates to develop clusters of storms from the \nInternational Border and southeastward across the aforementioned \nregion. With the heat we're expecting, there will be a cap to \novercome, which seems most likely to occur in the late afternoon to \nevening hours, but the potential for the cap to not break is there, \nlimiting our PoP forecast for now. Storms will likely be pretty \nquick if they develop, cruising from northwest to southeast with a \ndamaging wind and large hail threat. Our entire area is in a Slight \nRisk (level 2/5) for Sunday. As additional upper level shortwaves \ncontinue to move along the edge of the high pressure, some storms \ncould continue to form and propagate over the MN Arrowhead into NW \nWI Monday morning, where a Day 3 Marginal risk is in effect.   \n\nMonday and through the work week, temperatures temper slightly, but \nare still largely expected to be around to above normal with \nafternoon highs in the upper 70s to upper 80s and overnight lows in \nthe 50s and 60s. We remain in a somewhat stagnant pattern stuck \nbetween an upper level high over the Southwest to Central Plains and \nsemi-transient upper level lows moving over central to eastern \nCanada. This should continue to put us in position to see the \ninfluence of occasional ridge running disturbances which could bring \nspotty rain showers and thunderstorms. Some global models are \nsuggesting the potential for a slightly stronger upper level \nshortwave to bring a more potent system to the area late in the \nweek, but confidence is extremely low in any solutions that far out \nat this point.",
